WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.540
BRANDON ROYAL: Hi, I'm Brandon
Royal, solution manager

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.870
and technical strategist for
CI/CD and Anthos Solutions

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.100
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.740
I'm joined by my colleague,
Ben Good, solution architect

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.555
and DevOps expert,
also at Google Cloud.

00:00:17.555 --> 00:00:19.300
During today's
session, we'll discuss

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.160
some of the common
challenges that customers

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:25.000
see when it comes to modernizing
their CI/CD, especially around

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.983
hybrid cloud deployments
of that infrastructure.

00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:29.650
We'll review some of
the core principles

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.350
that we think about when
architecting and modernizing

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.510
CI/CD solutions with
enterprise customers.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.140
And finally, we'll
discuss how Anthos

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.030
can be leveraged
within your environment

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.690
to build more robust, consistent
pipelines across not only

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.450
on-prem as well as hybrid
cloud environments;

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.760
enable those pipelines
for not only application

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.760
configuration but also
policies and configuration;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.680
and ultimately help
your application teams

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.266
to deploy code faster.

00:00:56.266 --> 00:00:59.180
Now, CI/CD is not a new concept.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.030
It's been around for
at least a decade,

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.800
and a lot of the
tools and practices

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.780
really haven't
changed that much.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.220
What has changed
quite significantly

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.540
is the infrastructure
surrounding CI/CD.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.200
So specifically, we've
moved from a more imperative

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.820
infrastructure, where
we're describing

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.310
all the changes that we need
to do within our pipelines

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.920
to more of a declarative
infrastructure,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.500
not only for our applications
but also our underlying

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.110
infrastructure and
security policies.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.790
Now, speaking of
security, security

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.010
often has a very
different operating model

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.877
from developers and operations.

00:01:31.877 --> 00:01:33.460
And we see that as
a point of friction

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.760
with a lot of our customers.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.170
And then we also are seeing
very inconsistent tools

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:42.323
between on-premises deployments
of CI/CD and cloud deployments.

00:01:42.323 --> 00:01:43.990
Many of our customers
describe that they

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:45.700
have one very
different set of tools

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.360
for managing and deploying
the test-built automation

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.280
for their Cloud-based
applications

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.390
and a very different
one for on-premises.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.530
And we all know there's
manual processes that

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.190
are involved in especially
configuration changes

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.510
across environments.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.220
And we find that's
a huge issue when

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.860
creating those
inconsistencies, especially

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.522
between multiple
cloud environments.

00:02:10.522 --> 00:02:12.730
Now, DevOps Research and
Assessment, the organization

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.760
that brings you the
state of DevOps support,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.510
published some really
interesting findings

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.180
when it comes to
DevOps performance.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.040
This report has tons
and tons of details,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.540
but some of the
most important parts

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.180
are DevOps'
performance is directly

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.560
correlated with
organizational performance.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.020
So improving the performance of
your CI/CD pipelines, the pace

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.150
at which you're
deploying your code,

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.580
time in which it takes you
to recover from any service

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.560
degradation is
directly correlated

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.870
to your organization's
performance.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:42.810
Also, the selection
of cloud-based tools

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.670
have a very meaningful
impact in your ability

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.665
to be a high performer within
those key DevOps metrics.

00:02:48.665 --> 00:02:50.290
And the last in this
course isn't going

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.790
to be surprising to anyone.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.820
Organizations that
are using more slow

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.800
change management
approval processes

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.970
using standard ticketing-based
methods often don't perform

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.420
as well as those that are using
more GitOps-based methods.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.750
And that's what we're going to
talk a little bit about today.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.090
Now, when we think
about modernizing CI/CD,

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.560
we think about these
five principles.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.020
First, we look at security.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.760
So security is not
only in terms of how

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.610
we create a secure
pipeline, but also

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.610
how do we create a very
clear separation of duties

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.987
between developers,
operators, and security.

00:03:23.987 --> 00:03:25.570
And we'll talk
specifically about what

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.382
those responsibilities are.

00:03:27.382 --> 00:03:29.590
We want to make sure that
we're securing the pipeline

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.135
through vulnerability
management and attestation

00:03:32.135 --> 00:03:33.760
so we're able to
cryptographically sign

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.645
our images as they go from
build to test to deployment.

00:03:37.645 --> 00:03:41.170
And we want to manage the entire
process using robust access

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.830
control.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.440
Now, we talked a
lot about imperative

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.570
versus declarative
infrastructure.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.670
We want to manage all
of our configuration,

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.160
from application configuration
down to infrastructure

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.230
configuration, as code.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.410
That allows us to have a single
auditable source of truth

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.540
for all of our
applications and also

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.060
gives us a clean
Git-based interface

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.847
in which we can manage
all those configurations.

00:04:03.847 --> 00:04:05.680
We want to talk about
automation, of course.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.490
That's what's
CI/CD is all about.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:10.650
But we not only want to look at
automating our code deployment

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.150
process, but also our
security policy process.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.820
So the way in which we are
managing those security

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.930
configurations across
multicluster environments

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.170
is critical to automate as well.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.700
And we want to leverage
containers wherever possible,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.830
not only for the
applications-- because that's

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.380
what's going to
provide that isolated,

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.780
extensible environment--
but also for our actual

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.012
build infrastructure itself.

00:04:34.012 --> 00:04:35.470
Google has a
significant commitment

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.372
to Kubernetes'
open-source ecosystem.

00:04:37.372 --> 00:04:38.830
And we want to make
sure that we're

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.500
providing open standards-based
interfaces for all

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.570
of our CI/CD
infrastructure, allowing

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.790
you to leverage the existing
investments that you

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.060
have in place today.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.230
So what does that mean?

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:53.110
Ultimately, that
process of modernizing

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.590
your CI/CD practices
with Anthos is

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.690
going to help your developers
reduce burden and focus

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.930
on value-add tasks,
developing code and providing

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:03.400
to your customers.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.650
It will help you to
establish better governance

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.220
across your entire
CI/CD process and allow

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.980
you to share those best
practices between development

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.590
teams so one development
team can benefit

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.970
from the innovation of another.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:18.560
And finally, we
want to provide--

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.460
and this solution ultimately
will help to provide--

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.990
flexibility to deploy those
same configurations across cloud

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.570
environments as well as
on-prem environments.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.950
So when we think about CI/CD,
it's more than just the CI/CD

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:34.360
pipelines themselves.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.390
CI/CD pipelines are absolutely
important in our testing

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.440
process and our
deployment process.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.160
But we also want to think
about what's below the surface.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.430
And very similar to a
bridge structure where

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.140
below the roadway there's
all kinds of complexities

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:51.190
in managing the structure bridge
itself or the roadway itself,

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.580
we want to think about CI/CD
in a very similar way, where

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.410
the underlying structure
is our platform

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.930
configuration and our
management and our ability

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:02.140
to manage those configurations
across environments

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.900
and ultimately to orchestrate
and automate the underlying

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.300
infrastructure that
supports our applications

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.410
and our CI/CD pipelines.

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.740
The way that we do this
is we break down a problem

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.190
into components.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.730
So we talk about this in
terms of the CI/CD component

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:16.900
taxonomy.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:18.910
These are the major
components that anyone

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.285
needs to think
about when they're

00:06:20.285 --> 00:06:22.470
deploying a CI/CD process.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.060
And we want to ensure with
this methodology you're

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:26.770
able to leverage
existing components

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.960
that you might have
in place already,

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.480
including things like
application observability.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.450
You might have
robust CD pipelines

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:36.950
that you want to continue to
use or maybe even CI pipelines.

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:39.850
So this solution allows you
to plug in these existing

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.730
investments and manage
them on top of Anthos

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:46.410
for that ultimate consistency.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.420
When we talk about
various stakeholders

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.000
in the organization,
generally, we

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.410
talk about developers,
operators, and security.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:55.440
Each one has slightly
different goals

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.885
when it comes to modernizing
their approach to CI/CD.

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:01.950
The operations teams
are here to standardize

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.930
how application
teams are onboarded

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:07.320
and how those applications
are managed over time.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.020
The security team has a
new set of challenges.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:13.260
They ultimately need
to inject and propagate

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.950
all of those application
policies and configurations

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.590
for not only one environment
but multiple environments,

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:23.490
potentially spread across
multiple clouds and developers.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.200
We talked about
developers needing

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.568
to focus on developing
and providing code.

00:07:28.568 --> 00:07:31.110
So we want to reduce the burden
for those developers wherever

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.750
possible and allow them
to provide that value as

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.573
quickly as possible.

00:07:36.573 --> 00:07:38.740
One of the ways in which
we do that in this solution

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.700
is to provide
standard interfaces.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.450
And these are all open
standard interfaces

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:46.180
that we use in order to
plug in those existing

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.090
points into the Kubernetes
ecosystem wherever possible.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.780
The first is standard
Git-based interfaces,

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:56.980
and Git allows us to have
that Git-based or GitOps-based

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:01.780
process, in which we are
deploying all of our code

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:05.860
and configurations directly
through Git repositories.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.540
The second is
container-based images.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.170
Ultimately, we can describe
our application but also

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:14.620
our infrastructure using
standard OCI-based images.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.040
And last, these are all
ultimately Kubernetes objects.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.260
Anthos provides that
Kubernetes platform.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:24.220
And using things like Anthos
Configuration Management, that

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.140
allows us to manage
those Kubernetes

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:29.900
manifests across
multiple environments.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:31.820
So let's talk a little
bit about the workflow.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.880
some of these aspects will seem
very familiar to those of you

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.070
who are already
deploying CI/CD workflows

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.990
within your on-prem
environments.

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:40.655
The first is the
developer workflow.

00:08:40.655 --> 00:08:43.280
We want to allow our developers
to start with their application

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.260
code repository and use
a continuous integration

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.550
pipeline to build, to
test, and ultimately deploy

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:51.500
into a staged environment.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.290
This pipeline will build
our container images

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:57.911
and will push them into
a Container Registry.

00:08:57.911 --> 00:09:00.020
Now, next up is our operator.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:01.600
Our operator in
this case is going

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.520
to start with not the
application code repository

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.980
but a different repository
specifically designed

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.280
to share code best practices.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:11.390
Now, this repository
will contain

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:14.270
all of the shared standards
for various applications

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.190
that might be deployed
within the enterprise.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:18.620
And that operator can
use inheritance models

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.330
that we'll talk
about here shortly

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:23.750
to manage the configuration
delta between what's

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:26.120
deployed in the
developer's application

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.840
and what's managed in the shared
best practices repository.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.530
The operator will trigger a
pipeline specifically designed

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:36.120
for rendering all of
the configurations.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.930
So using a tool like
customize or like help,

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.840
we can actually take
multiple configurations

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.700
that might be shared
across our shared

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:47.120
best practices repository and
the application repository

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:50.810
and render them into fully
hydrated manifests in what we

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:53.075
call our environment repository.

00:09:53.075 --> 00:09:56.940
And that environment repository
is our single source of truth.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.600
That's where we can audit
all of our configurations.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.100
As we'll see in the
demo, we can see

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.810
all of these specific
configurations,

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.300
even down to the
hashes of our images

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.555
that are deployed in
various environments.

00:10:08.555 --> 00:10:10.170
And then last and
certainly not least,

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:14.020
our security, they need to use
an Anthos Configuration Manager

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.723
repository, or ACM,
repository to manage

00:10:16.723 --> 00:10:18.890
not only their infrastructure
configuration but also

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:24.080
their policies, which will be
then propagated to the clusters

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.250
using Anthos
Configuration Management.

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:31.090
Now, as far as the application
configurations are concerned,

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:34.580
again we want to start with a
shared set of best practices.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.480
So that allows us to use things
like a base configuration

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:39.670
for using customize,
in this case,

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:42.380
to describe what are the common
configurations that we might

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:44.630
have across our applications.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:47.110
And most applications,
there's lots of similarities

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:47.790
that might help.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.680
So we want to share those
best practices essentially.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:53.920
The second repository, the
Application Code Repository,

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.480
that's where we're going to
patch those configurations.

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:59.508
So anything above and beyond
those baseline configurations,

00:10:59.508 --> 00:11:01.300
that's where we're
going to specify those--

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:04.600
things like the images or any
additional port configuration

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:09.130
or other application-specific
configuration that might apply.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:13.150
Ultimately that will render a
fully hydrated manifest that

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:16.300
then gets stored within
our environment repository,

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.070
giving us that single
source of truth.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.130
So that's the
application workflow.

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:23.890
What about the
security workflow?

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:27.240
Security workflow is all
about policy management.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.890
So we of course manage our
underlying infrastructure.

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:34.270
But the core responsibility
of security in this context

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:37.610
is managing the propagation
of various policies.

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:40.060
This can be anything from
Pod Security Policies

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.890
to firewall policies.

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:45.880
Anything that describes the
behavior to essentially provide

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:50.400
guardrails to application teams
is what we define as a policy.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:53.820
Now, these are ultimately stored
directly as Kubernetes objects

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.170
and are handled by Anthos
Configuration Management.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.500
So under the hood, Anthos
Configuration Management

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.750
is listening to that repository.

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:04.050
And it allows our
security teams,

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.650
in coordination
with our operations

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:08.260
teams and development teams,
to push those policies

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:09.490
and configurations.

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:12.340
ACM takes care of rest,
taking those configurations

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:15.790
and propagating them
across those clusters,

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:19.330
allowing for full code review,
continuous integration,

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:23.427
and full integration into
developing an ecosystem.

00:12:23.427 --> 00:12:25.510
Now, to show us a little
bit about how that works,

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.027
I'm going to hand
it over to Ben.

00:12:27.027 --> 00:12:28.110
BEN GOOD: Thanks, Brandon.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:29.360
Let's take a look at the demo.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.100
Let's take a look at a
couple of the workflows

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:41.750
that Brandon was just describing
in the CI/CD on Anthos

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.710
presentation.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:46.190
So before we dig
into the exact--

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:49.430
into the workflows, let's take
a look at the GKE clusters

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:52.730
that we have deployed and
what we'll be using them for.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:54.890
So what we have four
clusters that we'll be

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:57.750
using to run our applications.

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:00.140
The first is a Dev Cluster.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:03.950
So in the demo, we use Skaffold
to automatically detect

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.810
the changes to the
application code

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:08.450
and then deploy them
into the Dev Cluster.

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:13.070
This will enable us to live test
our changes on the Dev Cluster

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:16.100
before we push them
into the repository.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:18.860
From there, when they're
pushed into the main line

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.420
of the application
code repository,

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:24.440
a deployment is
automatically kicked off,

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:28.420
which deploys our changes
to the staging cluster.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:33.010
Then, when we've validated our
changes on the staging cluster,

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:37.570
we can merge the
environment configuration

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:42.150
from the staging branch into
the main line branch, which

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:45.030
would trigger the production
deployment to our two

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.340
production clusters.

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:48.780
The fifth cluster
that we have out

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:51.690
here is used for
our shared services.

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:54.208
So on that cluster
we're running GitLab.

00:13:54.208 --> 00:13:56.250
And that's where we'll be
storing our source code

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:59.650
and running our CI/CD pipelines.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:02.610
So now let's pretend that
I'm an application developer

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:04.960
and I have a simple
Go application.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:09.650
If we take a look at that,
we see that it simply

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:11.990
outputs hello world.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:17.960
And my task is to update reading
this output from hello world

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:19.610
to something else.

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:22.760
So as a good developer,
what we'll do

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:24.710
is we'll start by
creating a new branch.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:28.110
We'll call it new greeting.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:31.000
And before I start
making changes here,

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:34.470
I want to get Skaffold started.

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:39.060
So it's connected up to
our development cluster.

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:41.680
Let's first make sure that
we're in the right context here.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:54.120
So Skaffold is going to
watch our source code.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:56.030
And as it changes,
it's automatically

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.880
going to build new
versions of the container,

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:00.890
push them into
artifact registry,

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:06.980
and then run deployments on
those new container images

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:11.910
out to our development clusters,
so that way we can test.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:15.320
So we can see here that
Skaffold is up and running.

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:22.900
And if we preview, we can
see hello world, this output.

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:26.360
So now we're ready to get
started with our changes.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:27.880
So we'll go to our editor here.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:34.585
So we're going to
say, hello Google.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:45.590
So that'll be our new greeting.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:48.380
So we can see, as I've
changed back to Cloud Shell,

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:50.670
that Skaffold has
detected that change

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:52.820
and is already starting
to rebuild the image.

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:57.630
Now the image has been deployed.

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:03.570
So if we refresh in our browser,
there is our new greeting.

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:04.960
And that looks good.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:09.300
So we're ready to push our
changes into our source control

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:12.592
system and kick off
the CI/CD pipelines.

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:16.830
Let's stop Skaffold here.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:26.990
And we'll commit our changes.

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:28.360
And we'll push them out.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:41.300
So now, if we go
over into GitLab

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:45.090
and we log in as
the developer here--

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:46.290
me, in this case--

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:58.793
we can see that I just
pushed a new branch.

00:16:58.793 --> 00:17:00.585
So let's go out and
create a merge request.

00:17:12.089 --> 00:17:14.119
So at this point,
what's happening is

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:17.339
we're running a CI
pipeline on our branch.

00:17:17.339 --> 00:17:20.099
And we are running tests.

00:17:20.099 --> 00:17:22.859
We're going to
hydrate the manifest.

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:25.720
And we're going to validate
the manifest as well.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:30.060
So we're running a
subset of the stages

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:32.490
that we'll run later
on, so everything

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:35.350
except for the deployment
part of the pipeline.

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:37.050
So this is giving
us the opportunity

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:40.800
to validate any manifest
changes that we might have made

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:43.950
in the application code
repository, as well

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:45.660
as any application changes.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.140
And we'll just have GitLab
merge when it succeeds.

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:58.445
We can see through the comments
here that everything is merged.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:04.240
If we look at our
CI/CD pipelines,

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:07.220
we're now running
another pipeline.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:10.660
So in this one, we're
running tests again.

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:13.900
We're going to
build our new image.

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:17.770
And we're going to hydrate
and validate configs.

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:19.630
And then we're going
to push those configs

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:22.060
into our environment repository.

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:24.320
So while we wait
for this to run,

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:27.440
let's take a quick
look at our repository

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.970
and what we have in here.

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:33.920
So in here we have our
source code, so our main.go

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:34.820
with our greeting.

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:36.560
We have our Dockerfile.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:40.100
And then we have some
Kubernetes manifests.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:45.728
So these manifests are the
changes that we want to make.

00:18:45.728 --> 00:18:47.645
So in this case, we're
setting the environment

00:18:47.645 --> 00:18:50.910
to dev for each environment.

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:55.737
And if we look at
staging or production,

00:18:55.737 --> 00:18:57.820
we can see that we're also
setting the environment

00:18:57.820 --> 00:18:58.780
to prod.

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:02.170
So these changes are
getting merged together

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:07.090
with the common
shared best practices

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:13.760
that are stored in this
shared customized basis.

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:16.210
So here's our shared basis.

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:23.440
And this is a fuller manifest,
so those environment settings

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:27.170
are getting merged into this.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:31.830
And what the CI pipeline,
the CI/CD pipeline, is doing

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:36.240
is it is going to
commit those manifests,

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:40.200
push those manifests into
the stadium branch here.

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:41.950
So it's still running
at this point.

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:10.190
So our pipeline is now finished.

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:16.630
So if we go and look at
our environment repository

00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:19.390
in our staging
branch, we can see

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:23.170
that we just pushed new configs,
both staging and production

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:24.010
YAMLs.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:25.850
We hydrate them all
at the same time

00:20:25.850 --> 00:20:31.985
so they're consistent and see
they are all fully hydrated

00:20:31.985 --> 00:20:32.485
manifest.

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:40.740
What that push did is that
push triggered a pipeline

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:45.120
to deploy those manifests to
our staging Kubernetes cluster.

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:51.560
And that's now done.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:21:03.160
So if we switch
back to Cloud Shell,

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:06.850
now we can see what we have
running in our namespace.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:11.430
And our application
is now running,

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.470
and it's been running
for 24 seconds.

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:17.730
So we have deployed our
changes to our staging cluster.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:23.770
Now we'll quickly promote those
changes from the staging branch

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:25.060
into the main line branch.

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:29.217
And that will trigger the
production deployment.

00:21:29.217 --> 00:21:30.050
So we'll go in here.

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:31.342
We'll make a new merge request.

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:56.780
We'll merge those.

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:02.780
If we take a quick peek
at the changes here,

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:05.810
we can see that all we
really changed was the hash.

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:14.770
But that merge request has
now triggered the deployment

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:16.640
to production.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:19.780
So we'll switch to our
prod central context.

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:34.200
And we can see that our
new pods have been running

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:37.230
for 14 seconds, so our
deployment to our production

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:40.240
clusters succeeded.

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:43.060
Now for the next workflow.

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:47.150
As the developer, I've been
talking with the Hipster Shop

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:48.490
front end team.

00:22:48.490 --> 00:22:51.700
They also have their application
running on our cluster,

00:22:51.700 --> 00:22:55.330
and they would like
to be able to connect

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:58.850
to our Go application that
we've been working with.

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:02.350
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:07.360
going to switch over to the
Anthos Config Management

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:09.940
repository.

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:14.020
You can see here we have all of
our namespaces and our cluster

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:17.180
registries and that
kind of stuff in there.

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:18.850
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:23.320
going to make a quick
change to our network policy

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:29.310
to allow Hipster Shop front
end to connect to our Go app.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:31.970
So first, before we
get started here,

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:35.370
let's take a quick look at
our current network policies.

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:44.430
So we can see that
right here we have

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:47.820
just a default deny
for anything that's

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:50.850
not within our namespace.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:52.350
Now, what we're
going to do is we're

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:58.770
going to enable that for
the Hipster Shop front end.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:00.780
So let's start again
by creating a branch.

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:08.140
And then we'll jump
back over to our editor.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:09.980
And then here's
our network policy

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:13.160
that is sitting inside of our
namespaces and our managed

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:15.260
applications, so our Go app.

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:26.140
So this policy is going to allow
traffic from the Hipster front

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:30.620
end to connect to
our Go application.

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:31.320
We'll say that.

00:24:43.368 --> 00:24:44.660
And then let's push our change.

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:51.450
So our change has been pushed.

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:57.170
So if we jump back over to the
GitLab UI here, what we'll see

00:24:57.170 --> 00:25:00.650
is we just pushed a new branch.

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:03.990
So since I pushed that
branch as the developer,

00:25:03.990 --> 00:25:06.330
I'm going to create
a merge request.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:08.990
So we're going to allow traffic
from the hipster front end.

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:14.980
So we'll submit
that merge request.

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:20.450
Now, our policy has been set
up such that, as a developer,

00:25:20.450 --> 00:25:27.320
I'm not allowed to merge
requests into the mainline.

00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:31.240
So we're going to have to switch
over to our network admin.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:34.620
So now I'm going to be
our network administrator.

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:36.980
And we'll review
those policy changes

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:40.550
so that way we can
now merge that request

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:42.290
and then allow that traffic.

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:43.820
And while we're
doing that, you can

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:48.290
see that we've kicked off
another CI pipeline where

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:51.920
we're verifying the
changes that we're

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:54.810
making to our configuration.

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:59.930
So we're running CI on
our Anthos configurations.

00:25:59.930 --> 00:26:00.820
We'll switch over.

00:26:10.335 --> 00:26:11.710
And we have a
merge request here.

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:14.380
So now I'm in as the
network administrator.

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:16.300
And we see the
merge request here

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:21.190
from me wanting to allow traffic
from the Hipster front end

00:26:21.190 --> 00:26:23.650
into our Go application.

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:26.710
So while we were doing that,
all of our tests passed.

00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:30.320
So let's quickly
view our changes.

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:31.820
So there's our
changes that we made.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:33.630
They look good.

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:34.580
So let's merge them.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:42.390
And now we're going
to run CI on them

00:26:42.390 --> 00:26:46.930
again, just to make sure
everything is still good.

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:49.720
And when CI has
finished, we'll be

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:51.710
able to go out to our cluster.

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:55.310
And we'll see that this
change has been applied.

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:58.050
And we'll check it on both the
production stage and cluster.

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:03.160
CI is finished.

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:05.970
So now if we jump back
out to Cloud Shell

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:11.780
and we look at our
network policies again,

00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:13.970
now we see that we're
allowing traffic

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:23.240
from application Hipster front
end into our Go app namespace.

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:25.460
So that process--
as a developer,

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:27.680
I needed to make a
network policy change.

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:32.690
I made the change in the
Anthos configuration management

00:27:32.690 --> 00:27:33.680
repository.

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:36.470
And then that change was
reviewed and approved

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:39.900
by a network administrator.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:42.390
As we saw in the demo, we
had several applications

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:45.630
deployed across a number of
clusters, both preproduction

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:47.100
and production.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:49.748
Each application ran
in their own namespace

00:27:49.748 --> 00:27:51.540
or their own little
playground, so that way

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:54.930
they ran in an
isolated environment.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:58.750
In order to allow applications
to communicate with each other,

00:27:58.750 --> 00:28:02.400
we had to involve the
operations and security team.

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:04.950
So that way we could
update the network policies

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:06.960
for those namespaces.

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:09.480
And those policies
and configurations

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:12.360
were being managed
using ACM, automatically

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:15.035
being deployed and reconciled
across all the clusters.

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:21.500
Now, as a developer,
they can focus

00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:25.170
on using a GitOps workflow.

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:29.310
So developers can build new
features in feature branches.

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:31.920
And using tools like
Skaffold, those changes

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:35.220
can be automatically deployed
to a development cluster

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:36.570
so they can be live tested.

00:28:39.650 --> 00:28:43.520
When those changes are finished,
a merge request is created.

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:46.450
And when they're merged
into the main line,

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:48.590
the staging deployment
is automatically

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:51.710
triggered by the push
of hydrated manifests

00:28:51.710 --> 00:28:55.468
into the staging branch of
the environment repository.

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:00.740
After those changes have
been validated on the staging

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:04.370
cluster, the operations
team or the developer,

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:07.640
depending on your workflow,
can create a merge request

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:11.210
to promote the configurations
from the staging branch

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:12.852
to the main line branch.

00:29:12.852 --> 00:29:15.060
And when those changes are
merged into the main line,

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:18.602
the deployment to production
is automatically triggered.

00:29:21.340 --> 00:29:24.180
So using the principles
that we described,

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:29.040
you are now able to build
upon a secure platform

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:30.690
where you're creating
and deploying

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:34.730
your policy using Anthos
Configuration Manager.

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:37.770
You're storing that
configuration as code.

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:40.680
So you automatically
get the tracking

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:44.400
and traceability
provided by Git.

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:47.270
And as you saw in the demo,
everything is highly automated,

00:29:47.270 --> 00:29:49.820
from the deployment
of application changes

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:52.190
all the way through the
configuration changes

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:56.590
that are tested and
deployed using ACM.

00:29:56.590 --> 00:29:58.340
We're also using containers.

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:01.150
So we're leveraging the
immutable nature of containers

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:04.350
to increase the reproducibility
of our deployments

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:05.950
in our infrastructure.

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:09.010
And we're leveraging
Anthos and Kubernetes

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:12.310
as an open source,
standards-based infrastructure

